1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:04,440
Maya: How Apple got started, what nearly stopped it, and what you can take from it.

2
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:05,946
Maya: That's today, on Day One.

3
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:16,940
Alex: This is Bits and Tickers.

4
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:18,640
Alex: What got built, and what it's worth.

5
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:19,090
Alex: I'm Alex.

6
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:20,960
Maya: And I'm Maya.

7
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:25,970
Maya: It's Sunday, November 22nd, and this is Day One: how one company went from nothing

8
00:00:25,970 --> 00:00:29,800
Maya: to a name everyone knows, and what you can borrow from it.

9
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:31,150
Maya: Today, Apple.

10
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:34,840
Maya: The history comes from its Wikipedia article, linked in the show notes.

11
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:37,420
Alex: Three chapters: The start.

12
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:38,300
Alex: The middle years.

13
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:39,200
Alex: And the later years.

14
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,140
Alex: Then The Playbook: what you can use yourself.

15
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:42,850
Alex: Let's get into it.

16
00:00:44,370 --> 00:00:50,140
Alex: It all kicked off on April first, 1976, in a garage in Cupertino.

17
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,700
Alex: First machine was pre‑assembled, no kit, just a motherboard that plugged into a TV.

18
00:00:57,190 --> 00:01:03,220
Maya: They called it Apple I, priced at $666.66, and it was ready to use.

19
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:04,020
Alex: I don't buy it.

20
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,990
Maya: The next day they dropped Wozniak's partner, Wayne,

21
00:01:07,990 --> 00:01:11,320
Maya: and brought in Markkula for a $250,000 stake.

22
00:01:11,890 --> 00:01:15,470
Maya: They launched the Apple II in 1977,

23
00:01:15,470 --> 00:01:18,620
Maya: adding color graphics and a keyboard in a plastic case.

24
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:19,200
Alex: Says who?

25
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:26,440
Maya: By 1980 they went public, selling 4.6 million shares at $22 each and hit a market

26
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:30,000
Maya: cap of $1.778 billion on day one.

27
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:33,240
Maya: You could just pre‑assemble a board and sell it to a retailer.

28
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:34,440
Alex: That sounds too easy.

29
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:35,920
Maya: Think about the timing.

30
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:43,300
Maya: The market was hungry for a plug‑and‑play PC, and they priced it at $666.66.

31
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:44,280
Alex: I don't buy it.

32
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:49,420
Maya: And then they sold shares at $22, raising $100 million.

33
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:53,200
Maya: The IPO capitalized on the hype and the tech boom.

34
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:54,060
Alex: The lesson?

35
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:58,640
Alex: Build something that plugs straight into a TV, and if you time it right,

36
00:01:58,640 --> 00:01:59,720
Alex: the market will bite.

37
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:06,940
Maya: So after the 1989 slump, Apple dropped a trio of cheaper machines that actually

38
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:07,670
Maya: sold well.

39
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:11,490
Alex: Yeah, they were the first to hit the mid‑price sweet spot,

40
00:02:11,490 --> 00:02:13,700
Alex: but they cannibalized the high‑end line.

41
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:18,600
Maya: Then they tossed a new notebook line that stole the market share and set a design

42
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:19,120
Maya: standard.

43
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:19,920
Alex: I don't buy it.

44
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:24,600
Maya: The operating system got a makeover too, adding color and better networking.

45
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:30,141
Alex: That upgrade pushed revenue up, but the overlap in product tiers confused buyers.

46
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:35,760
Maya: They kept pushing, launching a bunch of brands for different budgets—high end, mid,

47
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:36,260
Maya: and consumer.

48
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:38,840
Alex: Those overlapping lines were a mess, right?

49
00:02:39,130 --> 00:02:43,060
Maya: They even shut the long‑running line that had dominated for decades.

50
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:47,490
Alex: And the CEO shift meant new buyers were courting mergers,

51
00:02:47,490 --> 00:02:49,520
Alex: but talks with big firms fell through.

52
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:54,780
Alex: The real takeaway is the low‑cost line that actually sold, not the hype.

53
00:02:55,670 --> 00:02:57,090
Maya: You want to copy that?

54
00:02:57,090 --> 00:02:59,740
Maya: Drop a mid‑price tier that cannibalizes the premium.

55
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:00,600
Alex: Feels risky.

56
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:06,180
Maya: Timing was key—desktop publishing was cheaper on PCs, so Apple lost share.

57
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:10,140
Alex: So the lesson is to hit the sweet spot before the market price war.

58
00:03:10,910 --> 00:03:15,420
Maya: Remember, you need a unique design or feature, or you’ll just be another mid‑tier.

59
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:21,860
Alex: Apple snapped up Nothing Real for Shake and Emagic for Logic in 2002.

60
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:25,180
Maya: That was their first foray into music software.

61
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:28,160
Alex: They hit the market with GarageBand right after.

62
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:31,630
Maya: Then iPhoto joined the iLife suite that same year.

63
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:35,250
Alex: iTunes launched next year, 99¢ a song.

64
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:40,500
Maya: Five billion downloads by 2008, top retailer by 2010.

65
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:47,640
Alex: 2005 saw the Mac Mini, a $499 bite-sized Mac for Windows fans.

66
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,880
Alex: Boot Camp appeared in 2006, letting Windows run side by side.

67
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:57,480
Maya: Then the iPhone, App Store, iPad—each step pushed revenue up,

68
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:58,760
Maya: market cap over Microsoft.

69
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:03,280
Maya: The bite‑sized Mac Mini was a cheap way to steal Windows users.

70
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:06,120
Alex: That’s wild, but it was a gamble that paid off.

71
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:10,900
Maya: If you’re building a new platform, start small, cheap, and make it plug‑in‑friendly.

72
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:15,380
Alex: I think the key was timing—Windows was angry, Apple was hungry.

73
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:20,960
Maya: So you’d launch a low‑cost, modular device that hooks into an existing ecosystem.

74
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:24,680
Alex: Exactly, hit the sweet spot and the market will follow.

75
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:27,960
Alex: That's the story of Apple.

76
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:28,740
Alex: Time for The Playbook.

77
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:34,180
Maya: You start by copying the Apple I idea—build a single board that plugs straight into

78
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:35,460
Maya: what people already have.

79
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,020
Alex: It’s simple, but you’ll need to test the limits of that connection.

80
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:44,110
Maya: Then hit the second move: price it like the Apple II—mid‑price,

81
00:04:44,110 --> 00:04:46,760
Maya: enough to be attractive but not cheap enough to be a toy.

82
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:51,820
Alex: You’ll have to balance features so you don’t cannibalize your own premium line.

83
00:04:52,230 --> 00:04:56,420
Maya: The third move is to keep the ecosystem—let your device talk to something else,

84
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:58,320
Maya: like Apple’s plug‑in strategy.

85
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:02,550
Alex: You can’t just drop a fancy interface and expect users to buy.

86
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:06,170
Maya: Timing matters; luck helped Apple, but you can’t guarantee that.

87
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:11,840
Alex: This week, sketch a board that fits into a TV or a laptop screen and list the

88
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:13,420
Alex: components you’d need to source.

89
00:05:14,610 --> 00:05:16,380
Maya: And that's Day One.

90
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:19,740
Maya: Every story we talked about is linked in the show notes.

91
00:05:19,740 --> 00:05:21,200
Maya: Got a question for us?

92
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:22,800
Maya: The link to send it is there too.

93
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,860
Alex: Next up is First Tick, tomorrow.

94
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:31,540
Alex: Two reminders: our voices are made with AI, and nothing here is financial advice.

95
00:05:31,540 --> 00:05:33,820
Alex: From Bits and Tickers, I'm Alex.

96
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:34,380
Alex: Keep building.

97
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:34,860
Maya: I'm Maya.

98
00:05:34,860 --> 00:05:35,920
Maya: And keep counting.
